/// Encodes `data` bits with a shortened binary BCH code capable of correcting
/// `t` bit errors. Encodes data bits with a binary BCH code correcting `t`
/// errors. The code is the shortened narrow-sense primitive BCH code of the
/// smallest block length `2^m - 1` that can carry `data.len()` bits (see
/// [`Bch`]); the result is the systematic codeword `data ++ parity`. Use
/// [`Bch::encode`] for explicit control of `(n, k)` and for error details. #
/// Arguments * `data` - The data bits (`0` or `1`). * `t` - The error
/// correction capability (corrects up to `t` bit errors). # Returns The encoded
/// codeword, or an empty vector if the arguments are invalid (empty or
/// non-binary data, `t == 0`, or no supported code is large enough).
inline Term bch_encode(const Term &a0, const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("bch_encode", args, 2);
}

