/// `crt(remainders, moduli)` returns the least nonnegative solution for
/// nonempty equal-length lists and positive pairwise coprime moduli. Large
/// systems can use a product and remainder tree on the active CPU pool. Chinese
/// Remainder Theorem: solves `x ≡ remainders[i] (mod moduli[i])`. Returns
/// `(first, period)` with `0 <= first < period`. The period is the least common
/// multiple of the moduli. Non-coprime moduli are supported. # Errors The
/// inputs must be nonempty, of the same length, and have positive moduli and
/// consistent congruences. The period must fit in `i64`.
inline Term crt(const Term &a0, const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("crt", args, 2);
}

/// `crt_general(remainders, moduli)` returns `list(first, period)`, with `0 <=
/// first < period` and `period` the least common multiple. Lists must be
/// nonempty and equal in length, with compatible congruences and positive
/// moduli. Remainders may be signed and moduli may share factors.
inline Term crt_general(const Term &a0, const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("crt_general", args, 2);
}

