/// Native convergence levin_condition_number; normalized dimensionless
/// arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum
/// arguments use exact Rust variant names. Condition number `kappa = sum_j
/// |w_j|` at the highest order the term count supports. [`LevinVariant::V`]
/// consumes one extra look-ahead term. The transform is a weighted mean of the
/// partial sums, `L = sum_j w_j s_j` with `sum_j w_j = 1`. Those weights are
/// the solution of the Vandermonde-type system in the nodes `beta + n + j` that
/// the Fessler-Ford-Smith recurrence inverts implicitly, so `kappa` — the
/// 1-norm of that solution — is the factor by which rounding in `s_j` is
/// amplified into the estimate, and a lower bound for the 1-norm condition
/// number of the system. The accuracy floor of the transform is `kappa * eps`.
/// `kappa` depends on the series, which is why `terms` is an argument:
/// alternating terms give a convex combination with `kappa = 1`, while positive
/// logarithmically convergent terms give weights of alternating sign whose
/// absolute sum grows by a factor `~3.5` per order (for `1/(n+1)^2`: `4.6e5` at
/// order 11, `2.3e8` at order 16). Returns infinity for a nonpositive or
/// non-finite `beta`, an invalid remainder in the required prefix, or a
/// singular or unrepresentable condition number. Fewer than two terms return
/// `1.0` by convention.
inline Term num_analysis_convergence_levin_condition_number(const Term &a0,
                                                            const Term &a1,
                                                            const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_analysis_convergence_levin_condition_number", args, 3);
}

/// Returns one dimensionless Levin condition number per available order, from
/// order zero. Infinite values indicate an undefined or unbounded condition
/// number. Measured terms may share any dimension, beta is dimensionless, and
/// the input budget is 4096 terms. Estimates the Levin weight condition number
/// for each order starting at zero. Each entry is the absolute sum of the
/// normalized weights for its order. This measures sensitivity to the partial
/// sums with fixed remainder estimates. A singular or unrepresentable order
/// contributes infinity at its original position. The profile ends at the first
/// invalid remainder estimate. [`LevinVariant::V`] uses one additional term
/// beyond each order's prefix. A nonpositive or non-finite `beta` gives an
/// empty profile. The recurrence uses quadratic time in the valid prefix length
/// and linear workspace in the number of terms. ``` use
/// rssn::kernels::convergence::LevinVariant; use
/// rssn::kernels::convergence::levin_condition_numbers; let profile =
/// levin_condition_numbers(&[1.0, -0.5, 0.25], LevinVariant::T, 1.0);
/// assert_eq!(profile.len(), 3); assert!(profile.iter().all(|kappa| (kappa
/// - 1.0).abs() < 1e-14)); ```
inline Term num_analysis_convergence_levin_condition_numbers(const Term &a0,
                                                             const Term &a1,
                                                             const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_analysis_convergence_levin_condition_numbers", args, 3);
}

/// Native convergence levin_sum_lazy; normalized dimensionless arguments unless
/// the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use
/// exact Rust variant names. Sums a series whose terms arrive lazily, one at a
/// time. Pulls at most `max_terms` items from `terms` — an unbounded generator
/// is fine — and transforms the terms directly with [`levin_transform_terms`],
/// so the acceleration never depends on differencing rounded partial sums.
/// `beta` is the shift of the remainder estimate (`1.0` is usual). Returns
/// `(estimate, error_estimate)`, chosen by the same
/// smallest-successive-difference rule as [`levin_sum`]. # Errors Returns an
/// error if `max_terms` is below four, if the iterator yields fewer than four
/// terms, if a term is not finite, or if no finite transform can be formed.
inline Term num_analysis_convergence_levin_sum_lazy(const Term &a0,
                                                    const Term &a1,
                                                    const Term &a2,
                                                    const Term &a3) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3};
  return apply("num_analysis_convergence_levin_sum_lazy", args, 4);
}

/// Native convergence levin_transform; normalized dimensionless arguments
/// unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments
/// use exact Rust variant names. Levin-type sequence transformation of the
/// partial sums `s_0, s_1, ...`. With the term `a_n = s_n - s_{n-1}` (`a_0 =
/// s_0`) and the remainder estimate `omega_n` of the chosen [`LevinVariant`],
/// the transform `L_k^{(0)}` is computed with the stable Fessler-Ford-Smith
/// recurrence for numerator and denominator, `L_k^{(n)} = N_k^{(n)} /
/// D_k^{(n)}` with `N_{k+1}^{(n)} = N_k^{(n+1)} - (beta+n)(beta+n+k)^{k-1} /
/// (beta+n+k+1)^k N_k^{(n)}`. Order zero uses the first partial sum and later
/// orders use more terms. `beta` is the shift (`1.0` is the usual choice). The
/// result lists finite estimates in order and omits any non-finite estimates.
/// The terms are recovered by differencing, which cancels the leading digits
/// that consecutive partial sums share; when the terms are available directly,
/// [`levin_transform_terms`] avoids that loss.
inline Term num_analysis_convergence_levin_transform(const Term &a0,
                                                     const Term &a1,
                                                     const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_analysis_convergence_levin_transform", args, 3);
}

/// Native convergence levin_transform_terms; normalized dimensionless arguments
/// unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments
/// use exact Rust variant names. Levin-type transformation of the terms `a_0,
/// a_1, ...` themselves. Same transform as [`levin_transform`], but the terms
/// are used as given instead of being recovered from rounded partial sums by
/// `a_n = s_n - s_{n-1}`. That difference throws away the leading digits `s_n`
/// and `s_{n-1}` share — most of them late in a slowly converging series — so
/// this entry point keeps the full precision of the terms. The partial sums are
/// accumulated in the order the terms are given. This is the entry point for a
/// series whose terms are known in closed form or produced one at a time (see
/// [`levin_sum_lazy`]).
inline Term num_analysis_convergence_levin_transform_terms(const Term &a0,
                                                           const Term &a1,
                                                           const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_analysis_convergence_levin_transform_terms", args, 3);
}

/// Converts the fixed shape [x, y, z] to [radius, polar angle from +z, azimuth
/// from +x]. Express all lengths in one reference and use radians for both
/// angles. The polar angle is zero at the origin. Converts Cartesian `[x, y,
/// z]` to spherical `[r, theta, phi]`. `theta` is measured from the positive z
/// axis in `[0, π]` and `phi` is the azimuth from the positive x axis in `[-π,
/// π]` (both in radians). With horizontal length `h = hypot(x, y)`, the polar
/// angle is `atan2(h, z)`. It is zero at the origin. Finite coordinates can
/// produce an infinite radius if the norm overflows, with both angles still
/// defined. An infinite z component produces a NaN polar angle.
inline Term num_analysis_coordinates_cartesian_to_spherical(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_analysis_coordinates_cartesian_to_spherical", args, 1);
}

