/// Native antithetic Monte Carlo with physical compiled-function references and
/// bounded pair count. Antithetic-variate Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]`.
/// Each of the `pairs` iterations draws one uniform point `u` and evaluates the
/// integrand at both `u` and its reflection `1 - u` in every axis; the
/// estimator is the mean of the `pairs` pair averages, so it costs `2 * pairs`
/// evaluations. Both points are marginally uniform, hence the estimator is
/// unbiased whatever the correlation between them. For an integrand that is
/// monotone along the sampled axes the two evaluations are negatively
/// correlated and the variance is below that of [`monte_carlo`] with the same
/// number of evaluations; for an integrand that is symmetric about the centre
/// of the box the correlation is positive and the variance can be slightly
/// worse. The reported error is the standard error of the pair averages, which
/// is the correct standard error of this estimator and already accounts for the
/// correlation.
inline Term
num_analysis_monte_carlo_antithetic_compiled(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                             const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                             const Term &a4, const Term &a5) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5};
  return apply("num_analysis_monte_carlo_antithetic_compiled", args, 6);
}

/// Native serial Monte Carlo over physical bounds using an owned immutable
/// compiled program; same-unit coordinates, arbitrary output units, actual
/// native error/status and explicit sample/evaluation limits. Plain Monte Carlo
/// integral over `[lo, hi]` with a seeded generator; the error is the standard
/// error of the mean.
inline Term num_analysis_monte_carlo_compiled(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                              const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                              const Term &a4, const Term &a5) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5};
  return apply("num_analysis_monte_carlo_compiled", args, 6);
}

/// True native parallel Monte Carlo over physical bounds using an owned
/// immutable compiled program; same-unit coordinates, arbitrary output units,
/// actual native error/status and explicit sample/evaluation limits. Parallel
/// [`monte_carlo`]: every block of 4096 samples has its own generator stream
/// (seeded from `seed` and the block index), the block sums are combined in
/// block order. Deterministic for a given `seed` and `n` whatever the thread
/// count; the samples differ from the serial single-stream function.
inline Term num_analysis_monte_carlo_parallel(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                              const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                              const Term &a4, const Term &a5) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5};
  return apply("num_analysis_monte_carlo_parallel", args, 6);
}

/// Native stratified Monte Carlo with checked exponential grid/sample cost and
/// independent physical output units. Stratified Monte Carlo integral over
/// `[lo, hi]`. Every axis is cut into `strata_per_axis` equal cells, so the box
/// has `strata_per_axis^dim` strata and each stratum receives `per_stratum`
/// uniform samples. The estimator is the volume-weighted sum of the stratum
/// means and the error is the root of the sum of the squared stratum standard
/// errors, which is unbiased and never assumes that the strata are equally
/// variable. Stratification removes the between-stratum part of the variance,
/// so it pays when the integrand is smooth on the scale of a cell (the
/// within-cell variance falls like the square of the cell width) and buys
/// nothing when the integrand varies inside every cell, as a discontinuity
/// finer than the grid does. The cost is exponential in the dimension:
/// `strata^dim * per_stratum` evaluations. Arguments below one are treated as
/// one. Returns `converged = false` with a `NaN` value and no evaluations when
/// `strata_per_axis^dim * per_stratum` does not fit in a `usize`.
inline Term num_analysis_monte_carlo_stratified_compiled(
    const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
    const Term &a4, const Term &a5, const Term &a6) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6};
  return apply("num_analysis_monte_carlo_stratified_compiled", args, 7);
}

