/// Actual native sign-change event detection on the native piecewise-linear
/// stochastic path; this does not claim to detect unobserved between-node
/// process crossings. Programs receive coherent physical time/state components.
/// Detects sign changes of `g_i(t, y)` along a path. Between two consecutive
/// nodes the path is the chord of [`Path::eval`]; a crossing is a sign change
/// of `g` at the two nodes in an allowed direction (falling `g0 > 0 >= g1`,
/// rising `g0 < 0 <= g1`, the kernel's convention for ODE events), and its time
/// is the root of `g` along the chord, found by bisection. A node where `g` is
/// exactly zero counts once (as the end of the step that reaches it). Only the
/// discretised path is watched: the continuous process may cross and return
/// between two nodes, which no discrete scheme can see. Hits come in time
/// order. If an event is `terminal`, the returned path ends at the first
/// terminal hit (the hit is appended as its last node) and later hits are
/// dropped. The returned path is the input when nothing terminates.
