/// Logistic bifurcation samples in normalized coordinates; transient and
/// retained iterations share a finite work budget. Returns columns r and x.
/// Generates data for a bifurcation diagram of the logistic map. For each value
/// of r, the map is iterated and the final values after discarding transients
/// are recorded. # Arguments * `r_range` - The range of r values `(r_min,
/// r_max)`. * `num_r_values` - Number of r values to sample. * `transient` -
/// Number of initial iterations to discard. * `num_points` - Number of points
/// to record after transient. * `x0` - Initial x value. # Returns A `Vec` of
/// `(r, x)` pairs for plotting the bifurcation diagram.
inline Term num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_logistic_bifurcation(
    const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
    const Term &a4) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4};
  return apply("num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_logistic_bifurcation", args, 5);
}

/// Iterates the logistic map. The logistic map is defined by: x_{n+1} = r *
/// `x_n` * (1 - `x_n`) It is a classic example of how complex behavior can
/// arise from simple nonlinear dynamical equations. For r between 0 and 4, the
/// dynamics range from stable fixed points to period doubling to chaos. #
/// Arguments * `x0` - Initial value (should be between 0 and 1). * `r` - The
/// growth rate parameter. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. # Returns A
/// `Vec` of x values representing the orbit.
inline Term num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_logistic_map_iterate(
    const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_logistic_map_iterate", args, 3);
}

/// Computes the Lyapunov exponent for the logistic map. The Lyapunov exponent λ
/// measures the rate of separation of infinitesimally close trajectories. For
/// the logistic map: λ = lim (1/n) * sum(ln|r(1-2x_i)|) Positive λ indicates
/// chaos. # Arguments * `r` - The growth rate parameter. * `x0` - Initial
/// value. * `transient` - Number of iterations to discard. * `num_iterations` -
/// Number of iterations for averaging. # Returns The estimated Lyapunov
/// exponent.
inline Term num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_lyapunov_exponent_logistic(
    const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2, const Term &a3) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3};
  return apply("num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_lyapunov_exponent_logistic",
               args, 4);
}

/// Computes the largest Lyapunov exponent for a 3D flow (e.g., Lorenz system).
/// Uses the method of tracking the evolution of a small perturbation. #
/// Arguments * `start_point` - Initial point `(x, y, z)`. * `dt` - Time step. *
/// `num_steps` - Number of integration steps. * `sigma`, `rho`, `beta` - Lorenz
/// system parameters. # Returns The estimated largest Lyapunov exponent.
inline Term num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_lyapunov_exponent_lorenz(
    const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
    const Term &a4, const Term &a5) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5};
  return apply("num_fractal_geometry_and_chaos_lyapunov_exponent_lorenz", args,
               6);
}

/// Computes the escape time for a single point in the Mandelbrot set. #
/// Arguments * `c_real` - Real part of the complex number c * `c_imag` -
