/// Solves the multidimensional Burgers equation by conservative Lax–Friedrichs
/// fluxes. Use a velocity-valued frame. The flat initial field is in units of
/// value_reference with x fastest, spacing in units of length_reference, and dt
/// in units of time_reference. Supply Periodic or Outflow for both faces of
/// each axis. The CFL bound holds separately on each axis. The returned field
/// keeps all three references. Advances Burgers' equation with a boundary
/// choice for each active axis. Uses the same Lax–Friedrichs flux on both sides
/// of an interface. A fully periodic grid conserves the sum of cell averages up
/// to roundoff. Zero-gradient faces allow the boundary physical flux to leave
/// or enter the grid. For the equal viscosity shares used here, a sufficient
/// monotonicity condition is `D * dt * max|u| <= h[a]` on each active axis,
/// where `D` is the number of axes. At fixed grid spacing, the one-step limit
/// as `dt` tends to zero retains Lax–Friedrichs averaging. # Errors An invalid
/// grid, boundary count, initial field, or step size, or a flux or state that
/// cannot be represented.
inline Term num_grid_physics_fvm_burgers_nd_with_boundaries(
    const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
    const Term &a4, const Term &a5, const Term &a6) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6};
  return apply("num_grid_physics_fvm_burgers_nd_with_boundaries", args, 7);
}

