/// Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field
/// references. Diffusivity is normalized by L²/T, reaction by1/T, source by
/// U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical
/// output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.
/// Refines the cells flagged in `marked` by conforming edge bisection. Every
/// marked tetrahedron is split at the midpoints of its six edges into eight
/// tetrahedra. The split is carried out as recursive bisection: a cell is cut
/// along its longest split edge (ties broken by vertex indices) and the two
/// halves are cut again until no split edge is left. Only midpoints of edges of
/// marked cells are inserted, so an unmarked neighbour is cut along exactly the
/// edges it shares with marked cells (**red–green closure**) and a cell without
/// a split edge is kept. Both cells sharing a face cut that face from the same
/// edge midpoints to the opposite vertex of the face, hence there are no
/// hanging nodes. Boundary facets inherit the marker of the parent facet
/// containing them. The refinement level of every produced cell is one more
/// than the level of its parent; cells that are not touched keep their level.
/// In 1D and 2D the same scheme splits a marked cell into 2 or 4 cells. Entries
/// of `marked` past the last cell are ignored.
inline Term num_grid_simplex_refine_marked(const Term &a0, const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("num_grid_simplex_refine_marked", args, 2);
}

