/// `num_inverse_beta_reg(a, b, p)` returns the regularized beta quantile at a
/// probability in [0, 1]. Shape parameters must be finite and positive. The
/// general inversion uses a bounded search and reports nonrepresentable forward
/// evaluations as domain errors. The inverse of the regularized incomplete beta
/// function. Returns the `x` in `[0, 1]` such that `I_x(a, b) = p`. Endpoints
/// and the one-unit-shape cases use their analytic values. Other cases bracket
/// the quantile in binary64. Reflection gives the upper-tail probability
/// directly from the forward kernel when `x > 0.5`. # Errors The shapes must be
/// finite and positive, and the probability must lie in `[0, 1]`. A
/// nonrepresentable forward evaluation returns an error.
inline Term num_inverse_beta_reg(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                 const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_inverse_beta_reg", args, 3);
}

/// Computes the inverse error function, erf⁻¹(x). Starts from Winitzki's
/// closed-form approximation and polishes with Halley iterations on `erfc(y) =
/// 1 - x` (for x ≥ 0.5, where `1 - x` is exact and the tail keeps full relative
/// precision) or on `erf(y) = x` otherwise.
inline Term num_inverse_erf(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_inverse_erf", args, 1);
}

/// Returns `y` such that `erfc(y) = p` for `0 < p < 2`. The support endpoints
/// return signed infinities. Inputs outside `[0, 2]` return NaN. Refinement in
/// the far tail uses a logarithmic residual.
inline Term num_inverse_erfc(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_inverse_erfc", args, 1);
}

