/// Actual native CSC sparse kernel with explicit entry reference, preserving
/// historical CSR function names without transposing logical rows/columns.
/// Physical matrix/vector domains and normalized tolerances are checked; work
/// and allocations are bounded. Solves a sparse linear system `Ax=b` using the
/// Conjugate Gradient method. This method is suitable for symmetric,
/// positive-definite matrices. It is an iterative algorithm that converges to
/// the exact solution in at most `n` iterations (where `n` is the matrix
/// dimension) in exact arithmetic, but is typically stopped earlier based on a
/// tolerance. The tolerance is an absolute bound on the Euclidean norm of the
/// residual `b - A x`, checked before the first iteration and after every
/// update. If the iteration limit is reached without meeting it, the current
/// iterate is returned anyway. faer 0.24 does ship a conjugate gradient
/// (`faer::matrix_free::conjugate_gradient`), but it tests a *relative*
/// residual, reports `NonConvergence` as an error and requires a positive
/// definite operator, so using it would change all three behaviours; the loop
/// is kept, iterating over the faer storage. # Arguments * `a` - The sparse
/// matrix `A` ([`SparseMat`]). * `b` - The vector `b` (`Array1<f64>`). * `x0` -
/// An initial guess for the solution `x`. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of
/// iterations. * `tolerance` - The desired tolerance for the residual norm. #
/// Returns A `Result` containing the solution vector `x`, or an error string. #
/// Errors Returns an error if the matrix and vector dimensions are
/// incompatible.
inline Term num_linear_csc_cg(const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2,
                              const Term &a3, const Term &a4) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4};
  return apply("num_linear_csc_cg", args, 5);
}

