/// Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates.
/// Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs
/// use the same declared reference basis. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a
/// general (non-symmetric) real matrix. The matrix is first balanced by an
/// exact power-of-two diagonal similarity, reduced to upper Hessenberg form by
/// Householder similarities and driven to real Schur form by the implicitly
/// shifted QR algorithm (Francis double shift). A complex-conjugate pair of a
/// real matrix therefore appears as a 2x2 diagonal block and is returned as two
/// complex eigenvalues with complex eigenvectors; the transformation is
/// accumulated, so the vectors belong to the original matrix. Eigenvectors of
/// the quasi-triangular factor are obtained by block back substitution. For a
/// symmetric matrix prefer [`eigen_symmetric`]: it is cheaper and returns real
/// orthonormal vectors. A defective matrix (a Jordan block, for instance) has
/// fewer independent eigenvectors than eigenvalues. This routine returns one
/// vector per eigenvalue, flags the result through [`Eigen::defective`] and
/// leaves the dependent vectors parallel; see the module-level limitations. #
/// Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input,
/// [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] when a Schur eigenvalue needs more than `200
/// n` QR sweeps.
inline Term num_linear_dense_eigen(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_linear_dense_eigen", args, 1);
}

/// Checked native linear calculation in normalized reference coordinates.
/// Physical tensors must be explicitly nondimensionalized; normalized outputs
/// use the same declared reference basis. Dense symmetric generalised
/// eigenproblem `A x = lambda B x` (`A`, `B` symmetric). For positive definite
/// `B` (Cholesky `B = L L^T` succeeds) the problem is reduced to the symmetric
/// `L^{-1} A L^{-T}`; the eigenvalues are real and the eigenvectors
/// `B`-orthonormal. For **indefinite** `B` the Cholesky reduction does not
/// exist, and the eigenvalues are those of `B^{-1} A` (LU solve, then the
/// general QR algorithm), which may be complex pairs; no eigenvectors are
/// returned then. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for mismatched or non-square
/// input, [`DenseError::Singular`] when `B` is indefinite and singular,
/// [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] from the QR iteration.
inline Term num_linear_dense_eigen_generalized_symmetric(const Term &a0,
                                                         const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("num_linear_dense_eigen_generalized_symmetric", args, 2);
}

