/// Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry
/// reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and
/// residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use
/// A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are
/// preserved. Symmetric generalised eigenproblem `A x = lambda B x` (`A`
/// symmetric, `B` symmetric, positive definite for the Lanczos path): the `k`
/// eigenvalues closest to `opts.sigma`. `solve(x, y)` writes `(A - sigma
/// B)^{-1} x` into `y`. Block Lanczos runs in the `B` inner product on `(A -
/// sigma B)^{-1} B`, which is self-adjoint there; `theta` maps back to `lambda
/// = sigma + 1/theta`. The returned eigenvectors are `B`-orthonormal and
/// `residuals` are the true norms `||A x - lambda B x||₂`. Convergence uses the
/// Ritz residuals of the transformed operator. The callbacks write the full
/// action of a linear map. For a purely real eigenvector the residual requires
/// one application of each operator. **Indefinite `B`.** The `B` inner product
/// only exists for positive definite `B`. The solver first estimates the
/// extreme eigenvalues of `B` by Lanczos ([`b_is_positive_definite`]); when `B`
/// is not (numerically) positive definite it falls back to
/// [`eigs_gen_shift_invert`] (Krylov-Schur on `(A - sigma B)^{-1} B`, any
/// nonsingular `B`), whose eigenvalues may be complex pairs and whose
/// eigenvectors are 2-normalised, not `B`-orthonormal. # Errors As
/// [`eigs_sym_block`].
inline Term num_linear_eigs_gen_sym_shift_invert(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                                 const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                                 const Term &a4, const Term &a5,
                                                 const Term &a6, const Term &a7,
                                                 const Term &a8) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8};
  return apply("num_linear_eigs_gen_sym_shift_invert", args, 9);
}

/// Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry
/// reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and
/// residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use
/// A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are
/// preserved. Jacobi-Davidson for the `k` eigenvalues of a general
/// **non-symmetric** real operator closest to the (complex) `target`, interior
/// eigenvalues included. The method works in complex arithmetic with a *partial
/// Schur* deflation: the search space stays orthogonal to the locked Schur
/// vectors `Q` (`A Q = Q R`, `R` upper triangular), harmonic Ritz pairs of the
/// pencil `(W^H V, W^H W)`, `W = (I - Q Q^H)(A - target) V`, are extracted with
/// the complex Schur decomposition [`complex_schur`], and the correction
/// equation `(I - Q~ Q~^H)(A - theta)(I - Q~ Q~^H) t = -r` (`Q~ = [Q, u]`) is
/// solved by complex GMRES, optionally right-preconditioned by `precond`
/// (`K^{-1}` for a real `K ~ A - theta`, applied to the real and imaginary
/// parts). Eigenvalues are the diagonal of `R` and the eigenvectors are `Q y`
/// with `y` the eigenvectors of `R`; `residuals` are true residuals `|A x -
/// lambda x|`. As in [`eigs_jd_sym`], iteration continues after `k` locks until
/// no unlocked candidate is closer to the target. # Errors
/// [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if a
/// projected problem fails.
inline Term num_linear_eigs_jd(const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2,
                               const Term &a3, const Term &a4, const Term &a5,
                               const Term &a6, const Term &a7, const Term &a8,
                               const Term &a9, const Term &a10) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, a10};
  return apply("num_linear_eigs_jd", args, 11);
}

/// Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry
/// reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and
/// residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use
/// A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are
/// preserved. Jacobi-Davidson for the `k` eigenvalues of a symmetric operator
/// closest to `target` (interior eigenvalues included). The search space is
/// expanded with the solution `t` of the projected correction equation `(I - Q
/// Q^T)(A - theta I)(I - Q Q^T) t = -r` (`Q` holds the locked eigenvectors and
/// the current Ritz vector), solved approximately by GMRES; `precond`, if
/// given, writes `K^{-1} r` for an approximation `K` of `A - theta I` and is
/// made consistent with the projector. Harmonic Ritz extraction picks the Ritz
/// vector with the smallest `|A - target|` residual direction, which is
/// reliable for interior targets where ordinary Rayleigh-Ritz values do not
/// converge to the nearest eigenvalues. Converged vectors are locked and
/// deflated. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`;
/// [`EigsError::Dense`] if a projected eigenproblem fails.
inline Term num_linear_eigs_jd_sym(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                   const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                   const Term &a4, const Term &a5,
                                   const Term &a6, const Term &a7,
                                   const Term &a8, const Term &a9,
                                   const Term &a10) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, a10};
  return apply("num_linear_eigs_jd_sym", args, 11);
}

/// Native eigensolver over a checked operator matrix, retaining explicit entry
/// reference scales. Eigenvectors are normalized native rays; eigenvalue and
/// residual references are returned separately (generalized eigenvalues use
/// A/B). Native algorithm identity, convergence reports and work limits are
/// preserved. Two-sided Jacobi-Davidson for the `k` eigenvalues of a general
/// real operator nearest to the (complex) `target`, with **left and right**
/// eigenvectors. Two search spaces `V` (right) and `W` (left), each
/// orthonormal, are expanded together. The projected pencil `(W^H (A - tau) V,
/// W^H V)` gives Petrov-Galerkin pairs: the Ritz value `theta`, the right Ritz
/// vector `u = V y` with residual `r = (A - tau - theta) u` orthogonal to `W`,
/// and the left Ritz vector `w = W z` with residual `s = (A - tau - theta)^H w`
/// orthogonal to `V`; the left vectors of the small pencil come from the
/// inverse of its right-eigenvector matrix, so the *two-sided Rayleigh
/// quotient* `theta = w^H A u / w^H u` is stationary (its error is quadratic in
/// the residuals, unlike the one-sided quotient for a non-normal `A`). The two
/// oblique correction equations `(I - u w^H / w^H u)(A - theta)(I - u w^H / w^H
/// u) t = -r` and the adjoint one for `s` are solved by GMRES. Converged pairs
/// are deflated *obliquely*: locked right vectors `Q` and left vectors `P`
/// (scaled so that `p_j^H q_j = 1`, mutually biorthogonal for distinct
/// eigenvalues) define the projector `I - Q (P^H Q)^{-1} P^H` that keeps both
/// spaces away from them. `apply` is `x -> A x`, `apply_t` is `x -> A^T x` (the
/// adjoint of the real operator). A pair is accepted when both residuals are
/// below `opts.tol * max(|lambda|, 1)`. The standard (not harmonic) extraction
/// is used, so `target` should lie near the wanted eigenvalues. # Errors
/// [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if a
/// projected problem fails.
inline Term num_linear_eigs_jd_two_sided(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                         const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                         const Term &a4, const Term &a5,
                                         const Term &a6, const Term &a7,
                                         const Term &a8, const Term &a9,
                                         const Term &a10) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, a10};
  return apply("num_linear_eigs_jd_two_sided", args, 11);
}

