/// Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and
/// ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against
/// retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced. Monotone
/// piecewise cubic Hermite interpolation (PCHIP, Fritsch-Carlson). The
/// interpolant is `C^1` and, unlike a plain cubic spline, preserves the shape
/// of the data: on every interval its values stay inside the range of the two
/// end ordinates, so monotone data stay monotone and a step is not overshot.
/// Third order in the node spacing where the slope has one sign, second order
/// at a data extremum, where the slope estimate is clipped to zero — the price
/// of shape preservation. Values outside the node range are extrapolated with
/// the first or the last Hermite cubic. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of
/// `(x, y)` tuples with strictly increasing `x`. # Returns A `Result`
/// containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` evaluating the interpolant. #
/// Errors Returns an error if fewer than two points are given or the abscissae
/// are not strictly increasing. # Example ```rust use
/// rssn::kernels::interpolate::pchip_interpolation; // A step: the monotone
/// interpolant stays within [0, 1]. let step = [(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 0.0),
/// (2.0, 1.0), (3.0, 1.0)]; let f = pchip_interpolation(&step).unwrap(); for k
/// in 0..=30 { let v = f(f64::from(k) / 10.0); assert!((-1e-12..=1.0 +
/// 1e-12).contains(&v)); } ```
inline Term num_linear_pchip_interpolation(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                           const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_linear_pchip_interpolation", args, 3);
}

