/// Approximates the leading singular triplets of a finite matrix with a seeded
/// Gaussian sketch. Oversampling widens the sketch and power iterations improve
/// separation of nearby singular values. The columns of u and v are
/// dimensionless. Multiply each entry of s by singular_value_reference to
/// obtain a singular value in the matrix's physical units. A rank-`k`
/// approximate singular value decomposition of an `m x n` operator given by its
/// products: `matvec(x, y)` writes `A x` (`x` of length `n`, `y` of length `m`)
/// and `rmatvec(y, x)` writes `A^T y`. The range of `A` is sampled with `k +
/// oversample` random vectors, sharpened by power iterations with
/// re-orthogonalisation between them, and the small projected matrix is
/// decomposed densely. The cost is at most `2 (power_iterations + 1) (k +
/// oversample)` products. Extreme sampled columns are rescaled before
/// orthogonalisation. Singular values retain the scale of the original
/// operator. # Errors [`LowRankError::Shape`] when `k` is zero or exceeds
/// `min(m, n)`, or a product returns non-finite numbers;
/// [`LowRankError::NoConvergence`] when the dense decomposition of the
/// projected matrix fails.
inline Term num_linear_randomized_svd(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                      const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                      const Term &a4) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4};
  return apply("num_linear_randomized_svd", args, 5);
}

