/// Sample standard deviation. Computes the standard deviation of a slice of
/// data. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The
/// standard deviation of the data as an `f64`.
inline Term num_std_dev(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_std_dev", args, 1);
}

/// Fits a Cox proportional-hazards model for observed times, event flags and
/// finite dimensionless covariate rows without an intercept. Select breslow or
/// efron for tied events. Returns portable coefficients and fit diagnostics.
/// Fits a Cox proportional-hazards model by Newton-Raphson on the partial
/// likelihood, with step halving when a full step lowers the likelihood. `x`
/// holds one row of covariates per observation, `time` the observed times and
/// `event` whether the observation ended in the event. The iteration stops when
/// the largest absolute change of a coefficient falls below `tol` or after
/// `max_iter` iterations; the fit reports which happened through
/// [`CoxFit::converged`]. # Errors Returns an error for empty or mismatched
/// inputs, non-finite values, an empty design matrix, a covariate column with
/// zero variance, a sample in which every observation is censored, a singular
/// observed information matrix, or a non-positive `max_iter` or `tol`.
inline Term num_survival_fit_cox(const Term &a0, const Term &a1, const Term &a2,
                                 const Term &a3, const Term &a4,
                                 const Term &a5) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5};
  return apply("num_survival_fit_cox", args, 6);
}

/// Estimates the Kaplan–Meier curve and log-log confidence bounds. Event flags
/// distinguish observed events from censoring. The estimate retains the input
/// time dimension. Kaplan-Meier product-limit estimate of the survival
/// function. `time` holds the observed times and `event` is true when the
/// observation ended in the event of interest and false when it was censored.
/// The inputs are not modified; the estimate is computed on an internal sorted
/// copy. `conf` is the two-sided confidence level and must lie strictly between
/// 0 and 1. # Errors Returns an error for empty or mismatched inputs,
/// non-finite times, a confidence level outside `(0, 1)`, or a sample in which
/// every observation is censored.
inline Term num_survival_kaplan_meier(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                      const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_survival_kaplan_meier", args, 3);
}

/// Returns the first measured time at or below one-half survival. Returns None
/// if the fitted estimate never crosses one-half. Smallest observed time at
/// which the estimated survival drops to at most one half, or `None` when the
/// curve never reaches one half.
inline Term num_survival_median(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_survival_median", args, 1);
}

/// Predicts finite relative hazards exp(row*beta) from a portable fitted Cox
/// model and covariate rows with the fitted column count. Relative hazards
/// `exp(x . beta)` for new covariate rows. # Errors Returns an error when a row
/// does not have one entry per fitted coefficient.
inline Term num_survival_predict_cox(const Term &a0, const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("num_survival_predict_cox", args, 2);
}

/// Thin SVD A = U diag(s) V^T; returns u, s and v. Thin singular-value
/// decomposition using the bidiagonal SVD backend. Rank-deficient matrices
/// retain orthonormal null-space columns. No Gram matrix is formed, so small
/// singular values do not lose accuracy through squaring the condition number.
/// # Errors Returns [`DenseError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or
/// non-finite input, and [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] if the backend does not
/// converge.
inline Term num_svd(const Term &a0) {
  Term args[] = {a0};
  return apply("num_svd", args, 1);
}

/// Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget.
/// Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh:
/// a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules:
/// finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates
/// (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule:
/// tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include
/// value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations.
/// Callback failures propagate unchanged. Tanh-sinh quadrature with an explicit
/// refinement budget. A zero budget returns an unconverged estimate. Refinement
/// is capped at 20 levels to keep its exponentially growing node count bounded.
///
/// Higher-order: the function parameters are host callbacks.
/// Pass a `Callback` (see `rssn/callback.hpp`) or a `Value` for each
/// one; the result is a `Value`, not a `Term`.
template <class... Args> inline Value num_tanh_sinh(Args &&...args) {
  return detail::call_inferred("num_tanh_sinh", std::forward<Args>(args)...);
}

/// Undo repeated first differences using the original leading samples. Input
/// vectors share a dimension, which the result preserves. The returned length
/// is the sum of the input lengths. Inverse of [`difference`]. `initial` holds
/// the first `initial.len()` values of the undifferenced series, which fixes
/// the integration order. The returned vector has `d.len() + initial.len()`
/// entries. An empty `initial` copies `d` unchanged.
inline Term num_timeseries_integrate(const Term &a0, const Term &a1) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1};
  return apply("num_timeseries_integrate", args, 2);
}

/// Filter a homogeneous-dimensional linear Gaussian state-space model with
/// explicit initial mean and covariance. Return filtered means, predicted
/// means, filtered covariance matrices, and log likelihood relative to a
/// positive observation scale. Runs the Kalman filter for a linear Gaussian
/// state-space model `x_t = F x_{t-1} + w_t` with `w_t ~ N(0, Q)` and `y_t = H
/// x_t + v_t` with `v_t ~ N(0, R)`, started from `x_0 ~ N(x0, p0)`. # Errors
/// Returns a diagnostic for invalid shapes, non-finite data, nonsymmetric or
/// indefinite model covariances and an innovation covariance that is not
/// positive definite. Model covariances may be positive semidefinite.
inline Term num_timeseries_kalman_filter(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                         const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                         const Term &a4, const Term &a5,
                                         const Term &a6, const Term &a7) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7};
  return apply("num_timeseries_kalman_filter", args, 8);
}

/// Rauch–Tung–Striebel smoothed state means with explicit initial mean and
/// covariance. State components share a dimension and observations may have a
/// different common dimension. Returns one state per observation.
/// Rauch–Tung–Striebel smoothed state means. Runs the filter and then the
/// backward recursion `x_t^s = x_t + C_t (x_{t+1}^s - x_{t+1})` with `C_t = P_t
/// F' P_{t+1}^{-1}`, returning one smoothed state per observation. # Errors
/// Returns the errors of [`kalman_filter`], plus a message when a predicted
/// covariance is singular and the smoother cannot be formed.
inline Term num_timeseries_kalman_smooth(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                         const Term &a2, const Term &a3,
                                         const Term &a4, const Term &a5,
                                         const Term &a6) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6};
  return apply("num_timeseries_kalman_smooth", args, 7);
}

/// Compute means and variances for complete windows of a finite real time
/// series. Use ddof 0 for population variance or ddof 1 for sample variance.
/// The result contains empty vectors when the positive window width exceeds the
/// input length. Means retain sample units and variances square them. Computes
/// a mean and variance for every complete window in `samples`. Result `i`
/// describes `samples[i..i + window]`. The result is empty when `window`
/// exceeds the input length. Pass `ddof = 0` for population variance or `ddof =
/// 1` for sample variance. Samples are always required to be finite. Windows
/// are evaluated in linear time. Windows of at most 16 samples use two passes
/// around an anchor when the whole series has the same sign, its magnitudes are
/// within a factor of two, and their exponents leave enough headroom to square
/// the deviations. For other series and window widths, fixed blocks of window
/// starts combine suffix and prefix moments. Large inputs use the configured
/// worker pool, with identical arithmetic order for any worker count. # Errors
/// Returns [`RollingError`] for a zero window width, `ddof >= window`, a
/// non-finite sample, an overflowing variance, or host cancellation. # Example
/// ``` use rssn::kernels::timeseries::rolling::RollingMoment; use
/// rssn::kernels::timeseries::rolling::rolling_moments; assert_eq!(
/// rolling_moments(&[1.0, 3.0, 5.0], 2, 0)?, vec![ RollingMoment { mean: 2.0,
/// variance: 1.0 }, RollingMoment { mean: 4.0, variance: 1.0 }, ], ); ```
inline Term num_timeseries_rolling_moments(const Term &a0, const Term &a1,
                                           const Term &a2) {
  Term args[] = {a0, a1, a2};
  return apply("num_timeseries_rolling_moments", args, 3);
}

